    "evidence_text": "Glutaminolysis activates Rag-mTORC1 signaling.\n\nAmino acids control cell growth via activation of the highly conserved kinase TORC1.\n\nGlutamine is a particularly important amino acid in cell growth control and metabolism.\n\nHowever, the role of glutamine in TORC1 activation remains poorly defined.\n\nGlutamine is metabolized through glutaminolysis to produce \u03b1-ketoglutarate.\n\nFinally, cell growth and autophagy, two processes controlled by mTORC1, were regulated by glutaminolysis.\n\nThis may provide an explanation for glutamine addiction in cancer cells.\n\nGlutamine supports pancreatic cancer growth through a Kras-regulated metabolic pathway\n\nCancer cells have metabolic dependencies that distinguish them from their normal counterparts.\n\nAmong these dependencies is an increased use of the amino acid glutamine to fuel anabolic processes.\n\nIndeed, the spectrum of glutamine-dependent tumours and the mechanisms whereby glutamine supports cancer metabolism remain areas of active investigation.\n\nMoreover, knockdown of any component enzyme in this series of reactions also results in a pronounced suppression of PDAC growth in vitro and in vivo.\n\nPharmacological Blockade of ASCT2-dependent Glutamine Transport Leads To Anti-tumor Efficacy in Preclinical Models\n\nThe unique metabolic demands of cancer cells underscore potentially fruitful opportunities for drug discovery in the era of precision medicine.\n\nHowever, therapeutic targeting of cancer metabolism has led to surprisingly few new drugs to date.\n\nThe neutral amino acid glutamine serves as a key intermediate in numerous metabolic processes leveraged by cancer cells, including biosynthesis, cell signaling, and oxidative protection.\n\nCell-State-Specific Metabolic Dependency in Hematopoiesis and Leukemogenesis\n\nThe balance between oxidative and nonoxidative glucose metabolism is essential for a number of pathophysiological processes.\n\nThis finding suggests that fine-tuning the level of glycolysis may be explored therapeutically for treating leukemia while preserving HSC function.\n\nCa2+-permeable AMPA receptors regulate growth of human glioblastoma via Akt activation.\n\nA dominant-negative form of Akt inhibited cell proliferation and migration accelerated by overexpression of Ca2+-permeable AMPA receptor.\n\nTherefore, Akt functions as downstream effectors for Ca2+-signaling mediated by AMPA receptor in glioblastoma cells.\n\nThis novel pathway might give an alternative therapeutic target.\n\nControl of Nutrient Stress-Induced Metabolic Reprogramming by PKC\u03b6 in Tumorigenesis\n\nWe show that PKC\u03b6 deficiency promotes the plasticity necessary for cancer cells to reprogram their metabolism to utilize glutamine through the serine biosynthetic pathway in the absence of glucose.\n\nFurthermore, PKC\u03b6 and caspase-3 activities are correlated with PHGDH levels in human intestinal tumors.\n\nTaken together, this demonstrates that PKC\u03b6 is a critical metabolic tumor suppressor in mouse and human cancer.\n\nProteomic analysis reveals Warburg effect and anomalous metabolism of glutamine in pancreatic cancer cells.\n\nThe data demonstrated that metabolic pathways were altered in PANC-1, consistent with the Warburg effect.\n\nmTORC2 Regulates Amino Acid Metabolism in Cancer by Phosphorylation of the Cystine-Glutamate Antiporter xCT.\n\nMutations in cancer reprogram amino acid metabolism to drive tumor growth, but the molecular mechanisms are not well understood.\n\nThese results identify an unanticipated mechanism regulating amino acid metabolism in cancer, enabling tumor cells to adapt to changing environmental conditions.\n\nTargeted inhibition of tumor-specific glutaminase diminishes cell-autonomous tumorigenesis.\n\nGlutaminase (GLS), which converts glutamine to glutamate, plays a key role in cancer cell metabolism, growth, and proliferation.\n\nConversely, a Vivo-Morpholino directed at mouse Gls had no antitumor activity in vivo.\n\nTargeting metabolic remodeling in glioblastoma multiforme.",
